CAT = "sgresContract"; SEG = "CON"
Q = [
("A valid contract needs offer and acceptance, intention to create legal relations, capacity, legality and:",
 "Consideration or a deed", ["A lawyer as witness", "Registration with SLA", "A stamped receipt"],
 "A promise is binding only if supported by consideration or made by deed. Stamping and registration matter for enforcement and title, not for whether a contract exists."),
("The 3Ps that must be agreed for a property offer to be certain are:",
 "Parties, property and price", ["Price, payment and plans", "Parties, permit and plot", "Property, period and permit"],
 "An offer must identify the parties, the property and the price. Without these, there is nothing certain enough for acceptance to turn into a contract."),
("A letter of intent for a lease marked \"subject to contract\" usually means:",
 "No binding lease exists yet", ["The lease is binding on both sides", "The deposit is forfeited", "The tenant may move in"],
 "\"Subject to contract\" signals that the parties do not intend to be bound until a formal agreement is signed. A good faith deposit paid with it is usually refundable or set off as agreed."),
("When a buyer exercises an option to purchase within the option period:",
 "A binding contract forms", ["The seller's offer lapses", "The seller may still resell", "Only a licence arises"],
 "An option is the seller's irrevocable offer, kept open in return for the option fee. Exercising it in time and in the stated manner is acceptance, creating a contract of sale."),
("Breach of a condition, as opposed to a warranty, allows the innocent party to:",
 "Terminate and claim damages", ["Only claim damages, not end it", "Only rescind, never sue", "Wait until completion to act"],
 "A condition is a term going to the root of the contract, so its breach lets the innocent party treat the contract as ended and claim damages. Breach of a warranty gives damages only."),
("A term not written in the contract but read in by law or to give it business efficacy is:",
 "An implied term", ["An express term", "An exemption clause", "An indemnity clause"],
 "Implied terms come from statute, custom or what the parties obviously intended. Express terms are those actually stated, orally or in writing."),
("For an exemption clause to protect a party, it must first be:",
 "Incorporated into the contract", ["Printed in bold red type", "Approved in advance by CEA", "Registered against the land title"],
 "The clause must be part of the contract, for example in a signed document or by reasonable notice before the deal. Courts then interpret it and test it against unfair contract terms rules."),
("An ambiguous exemption clause is usually read:",
 "Against the party relying on it", ["In favour of whoever drafted it", "As void in every case", "In favour of the stronger party"],
 "Under the contra proferentem rule, unclear wording is construed against the party seeking to rely on it. A clearly worded clause may still be valid."),
("An indemnity clause in a tenancy agreement mainly serves to:",
 "Make one side cover set losses", ["Fix the rent for the term", "Allow early termination", "Set the agent's commission rate"],
 "An indemnity clause makes one party compensate the other for specified losses or claims, such as a tenant covering losses caused by its use of the premises."),
("A contract induced by a seller's misrepresentation is generally:",
 "Voidable", ["Void", "Valid with no remedy", "Illegal"],
 "Misrepresentation makes a contract voidable: the misled party may rescind it and may claim damages. A void contract never existed at all."),
("If, unknown to both parties, the property had already been destroyed when they contracted, the contract is:",
 "Void for mistake", ["Voidable for duress", "Valid and binding", "Frustrated later on"],
 "A common mistake about the existence of the subject matter makes the contract void. Frustration applies to events after the contract is made."),
("Which vitiating factor involves illegitimate pressure, such as threats, to make someone sign?",
 "Duress", ["Mistake", "Misrepresentation", "Frustration"],
 "Duress is illegitimate pressure that leaves no real choice, making the contract voidable. Undue influence is the related abuse of a relationship of trust."),
("A contract may be discharged by frustration when:",
 "A later event makes it impossible", ["One side changes its mind", "Prices fall after signing", "The agent leaves the estate agency"],
 "Frustration covers a supervening event, not the fault of either party, that makes performance impossible or radically different. A bad bargain or a change of mind does not frustrate a contract."),
("Why do courts often order specific performance of a contract to sell land?",
 "Each property is unique", ["Damages are always larger", "The agent asks for it", "Land has no market value"],
 "Because no two properties are the same, money may not be an adequate remedy, so a court may order the defaulting party to complete the sale."),
("After exercising an option, a buyer stops payment on the deposit cheque. This is most likely:",
 "A repudiation of the contract", ["A valid way to rescind", "A mere invitation to treat", "Frustration of the whole contract"],
 "Once the option is exercised a contract exists, so refusing to pay shows an intention not to perform. The seller may accept the repudiation and claim remedies such as forfeiting the option fee."),
("For a contract to sell land to be enforceable, it must be:",
 "In writing and signed", ["Witnessed by an agent", "Lodged with CEA", "Stamped before signing"],
 "The Civil Law Act requires a contract for the sale of land to be evidenced in writing and signed by the party to be charged. An oral agreement to sell land cannot be enforced."),
("Transferring the benefit of a contract to another person is called:",
 "Assignment", ["Novation", "Ratification", "Subrogation"],
 "Assignment transfers the benefit of a contract, while the burden stays with the original party. Novation replaces a party altogether with everyone's consent."),
("A court order stopping a party from doing a particular act is:",
 "An injunction", ["Specific performance", "Rescission", "Liquidated damages"],
 "An injunction restrains a party from acting, such as building over a right of way. Specific performance compels a party to do what it promised."),
("To succeed in negligence, a claimant must show a duty of care, a breach of it, and:",
 "Damage caused by the breach", ["Intention to cause harm", "A signed contract", "A criminal conviction"],
 "Negligence needs damage that was caused by the breach and is not too remote. Intention is not required, and no contract is needed."),
("A salesperson carelessly tells a buyer that a leasehold condo is freehold, and the buyer relies on it. This risks liability for:",
 "Negligent misstatement", ["Trespass to land", "Private nuisance", "Breach of trust"],
 "Careless advice given in a relationship where the listener reasonably relies on it can found a claim in negligent misstatement for the loss caused."),
("Singapore courts decide whether a duty of care exists by looking at factual foreseeability, then:",
 "Proximity and policy", ["Price and payment", "Intent and malice", "Consent and capacity"],
 "Singapore uses a two-stage test with a threshold of factual foreseeability: legal proximity between the parties, then whether policy reasons negate the duty."),
("The standard of care expected of a salesperson in estate agency work is that of:",
 "A competent salesperson", ["A senior conveyancing lawyer", "An ordinary untrained person", "The best valuer in the market"],
 "A professional is judged against a reasonably competent member of that profession. A salesperson is not held to a lawyer's or expert valuer's standard, nor excused as a layperson."),
("A salesperson enters a client's vacant unit without the owner's permission to take photographs. This is:",
 "Trespass to land", ["Private nuisance", "Negligent misstatement", "Breach of warranty"],
 "Trespass is a direct, intentional entry onto land in another's possession without consent or lawful authority. Damage is not needed for it to be actionable."),
("Persistent loud noise from a neighbouring unit that unreasonably interferes with an owner's enjoyment is:",
 "Private nuisance", ["Public nuisance", "Trespass to land", "Negligent misstatement"],
 "Private nuisance is an unreasonable interference with the use and enjoyment of land. Public nuisance affects the public or a class of it."),
]
